{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## WHERE the state lives, and WHO can read it — the containment question, answered\n\nRequested follow-up. All read-only; only digests and lengths were ever computed, never a value.\n\n### Location: REMOTE, in S3, encrypted at rest with SSE-S3 (not a CMK)\n\n`infra/terraform/versions.tf:13-19`:\n\n    backend \"s3\" {\n      bucket       = \"rebar-tfstate-896586841071\"\n      key          = \"rebar/prod/terraform.tfstate\"\n      region       = \"us-east-1\"\n      encrypt      = true\n      use_lockfile = true\n    }\n\nBucket posture, from the live API:\n\n    default encryption      SSE-S3 / AES256   (BucketKeyEnabled=false; SSE-C blocked)\n    public access block     BlockPublicAcls=true  IgnorePublicAcls=true\n                            BlockPublicPolicy=true  RestrictPublicBuckets=true\n    bucket policy           NONE (GetBucketPolicy -> NoSuchBucketPolicy)\n    bucket ACL              a single FULL_CONTROL grant to the account canonical owner\n    versioning              ENABLED\n\nThere is no local `terraform.tfstate` in the repo — the state is remote-only, so this is not a\nlaptop-file exposure.\n\nTwo things follow from the encryption line. It is **SSE-S3 (AES256), not a KMS CMK**, so there is\nno separate key policy and no per-decrypt CloudTrail data event to constrain or observe access —\n`s3:GetObject` alone is sufficient, and encryption-at-rest adds nothing against a principal who\nalready has that permission. And because there is **no bucket policy at all**, access is governed\n**solely by IAM identity policies** in account `896586841071`; there is no resource-side allow-list\nto narrow it.\n\n### Who can read it: contained to account-admin principals\n\nResolved with `iam simulate-principal-policy` against the exact state object ARN rather than by\nreading policy documents, so this is the effective decision, not an inferred one:\n\n    arn:aws:iam::896586841071:role/rebar-gerrit-instance-role   s3:GetObject -> implicitDeny\n    arn:aws:iam::896586841071:user/joe_frontier                 s3:GetObject -> allowed\n\n**The good news, and it is the load-bearing fact: the EC2 instance role CANNOT read the state.**\n`rebar-gerrit-instance-role` carries only `AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore` plus three narrow inline\npolicies (`rebar-gerrit-bedrock-converse`, `rebar-gerrit-cloudwatch-basic`,\n`rebar-gerrit-ssm-params-read`). So compromising the internet-facing box — the containers, Gerrit,\nthe MCP server — does **not** hand over the state file and its 23 cleartext secrets. The box can\nread the SSM parameters it is scoped to, which is the intended, far narrower grant.\n\nThe account holds exactly **one IAM user, `joe_frontier`** (an administrator, and the identity\nthese read-only checks ran under), plus `OrganizationAccountAccessRole` for cross-account org\nadmin. Every other role is an unrelated `lmn-*` application role.\n\n**Verdict on containment: CONTAINED, not exposed.** The reachable set is account administrators,\nwho can already read the underlying SSM SecureStrings directly. This is not a privilege-escalation\npath from the box, and it is not public. That materially lowers the urgency relative to the\noriginal filing.\n\n### The part that is NOT contained: 149 retained versions\n\n    aws s3api list-object-versions --prefix rebar/prod/terraform.tfstate -> 149 versions\n\nVersioning is enabled and there is no lifecycle expiry on noncurrent versions, so **every secret\nvalue that has ever passed through this state remains readable in an older object version**. The\nconsequence is the one worth acting on: **rotating a secret does not remove it from the state\nhistory.** A key rotated after a suspected exposure is still sitting in cleartext in a prior\nversion, and `s3api get-object --version-id` retrieves it. Any remediation that only fixes the\ncurrent state leaves the historical tail in place, so the tail needs its own decision — noncurrent\nversion expiry, or an explicit accept.\n\n### Net reassessment\n\nContained to admins, so not an emergency — but the 149-version tail means the exposure window is\nunbounded in time rather than a point-in-time snapshot, and SSE-S3 gives no key-policy or\ndata-event control over reads. Priority raised P2 -> P1 accordingly.\n\nNo remediation attempted, per instruction."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787961557038852001,"uuid":"0fca6275-7b2e-45e8-b120-5b4e7252462a"}